<section class="ph"><div class="ph-media"><img class="ph-img" src="/assets/img/photos/paperwork.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/paperwork-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/paperwork.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="798" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap ph-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><span aria-current="page">Claim glossary</span></nav><p class="eyebrow eyebrow-light">Reference</p><h1>Insurance Claim Terms Glossary for Michigan</h1><p class="ph-lede">47 terms you’ll meet in policies, estimates and appraisal awards, defined in plain English. Type in the box to filter.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><div class="filter"><label for="gloss-filter">Search the glossary</label><input id="gloss-filter" type="search" placeholder="Try “holdback”, “umpire” or “ice dam”" autocomplete="off"><p class="hint" data-gloss-count>47 terms</p></div><div class="az"><a href="#g-A">A</a><a href="#g-C">C</a><a href="#g-D">D</a><a href="#g-E">E</a><a href="#g-H">H</a><a href="#g-I">I</a><a href="#g-L">L</a><a href="#g-M">M</a><a href="#g-O">O</a><a href="#g-P">P</a><a href="#g-R">R</a><a href="#g-S">S</a><a href="#g-T">T</a><a href="#g-U">U</a><a href="#g-W">W</a><a href="#g-X">X</a></div><dl class="glossary"><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-A">Actual cash value</dt><dd>The value of damaged property at the time of loss, usually replacement cost minus depreciation. Many policies pay this amount first.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Additional living expenses</dt><dd>Increased costs of living elsewhere, such as rent and meals above normal, while a damaged home is repaired.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Amount of loss</dt><dd>The dollar value of the covered damage. In appraisal, this is the figure the award sets.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Appraisal award</dt><dd>The written decision, signed by any two of the two appraisers and the umpire, that determines the amount of loss.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Appraisal clause</dt><dd>The policy provision that lets either side demand appraisal when they disagree on the amount of loss. Michigan law requires it in property policies.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-C">Collapse coverage</dt><dd>Limited coverage for sudden collapse of a building or part of one caused by specific listed perils, such as the weight of snow or ice.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Competent and independent appraiser</dt><dd>An appraiser with the skill to value the loss and no financial interest or bias that would compromise their judgment.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Coverage A</dt><dd>Dwelling coverage for the house itself and attached structures such as an attached garage.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Coverage B</dt><dd>Other structures coverage for detached buildings such as sheds, fences and detached garages.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Coverage C</dt><dd>Personal property coverage for the contents of the home, such as furniture, clothing and electronics.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Coverage D</dt><dd>Loss of use coverage, which pays additional living expenses or fair rental value while the home can’t be lived in.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-D">Debris removal</dt><dd>Coverage for the cost of hauling away damaged property and debris, sometimes with its own limit.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Declarations page</dt><dd>The summary page of a policy listing the insured, property, coverage limits, deductibles and policy period.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Deductible</dt><dd>The amount the policyholder pays toward a covered loss before the insurer’s payment applies.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Depreciation</dt><dd>The reduction in value assigned to property because of age, wear and condition. It is the difference between replacement cost and actual cash value.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>DIFS</dt><dd>The Michigan Department of Insurance and Financial Services, the state agency that regulates insurers and takes consumer complaints.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Drip edge</dt><dd>Metal flashing installed along roof edges to direct water away from the fascia and into the gutters.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-E">Examination under oath</dt><dd>Sworn questioning of the policyholder by the insurer’s representative, which many policies allow the insurer to require.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-H">Holdback</dt><dd>The portion of a claim payment, usually recoverable depreciation, that the insurer keeps back until repairs are done and documented.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-I">Ice and water shield</dt><dd>A self-adhering waterproof underlayment installed at eaves and valleys to resist ice dam leaks, often required by code in cold climates.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Ice dam</dt><dd>A ridge of ice at the roof edge that traps melting snow, which can back up under shingles and leak into the home.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-L">Lake-effect snow</dt><dd>Heavy snow produced when cold air crosses the warmer Great Lakes, common along the west Michigan lakeshore and in the U.P.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Line of sight</dt><dd>An approach to matching that looks at whether repaired and unrepaired areas are visible together as one continuous surface.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-M">Matching</dt><dd>The question of whether undamaged materials must be replaced so repairs match in color, texture and appearance. Policy wording and the facts control.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>MBPIA</dt><dd>The Michigan Basic Property Insurance Association, Michigan’s FAIR Plan for owners who can’t get coverage in the voluntary market.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Mitigation</dt><dd>Reasonable steps taken after a loss to prevent further damage, such as tarping a roof or drying a wet basement.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-O">Ordinance or law coverage</dt><dd>Coverage for added costs to bring repaired property up to current building codes, subject to its own limit.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Overhead and profit</dt><dd>A contractor’s charge for business costs and margin, often 10% plus 10% in estimates, typically included when a general contractor is reasonably needed.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-P">Penalty interest</dt><dd>Interest owed on certain late claim payments. Michigan’s MCL 500.2006 provides for 12% per year in certain circumstances.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Percentage deductible</dt><dd>A deductible set as a percentage of the dwelling limit rather than a flat dollar amount, sometimes used for wind or hail losses.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Pitch</dt><dd>The steepness of a roof, expressed as inches of rise per 12 inches of run. Steeper pitches cost more to work on.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Proof of loss</dt><dd>A sworn statement from the policyholder describing the loss and the amount claimed, often required within a set time after the insurer asks for it.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Public adjuster</dt><dd>A licensed professional hired by a policyholder to prepare and negotiate a claim, usually for a percentage of the recovery.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-R">Recoverable depreciation</dt><dd>Depreciation the insurer withholds at first but pays once repairs or replacement are completed, under a replacement cost policy.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Replacement cost value</dt><dd>The cost to repair or replace damaged property with materials of like kind and quality, without deducting depreciation.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Reservation of rights</dt><dd>A letter in which the insurer continues handling a claim while reserving the right to deny coverage later.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-S">Scope of loss</dt><dd>The list of damaged items and the repairs needed to restore them, before pricing is applied.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Sewer and drain backup endorsement</dt><dd>An optional add-on that covers water backing up through sewers or drains, which standard homeowner policies usually exclude.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Square (roofing)</dt><dd>A roofing unit equal to 100 square feet of roof surface.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Suit limitation</dt><dd>A policy or statutory deadline to sue. In Michigan it is generally one year after the loss, tolled while the claim is pending.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Supplement</dt><dd>A request for additional payment after the first estimate, usually for damage or costs found once repairs begin.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Surface water</dt><dd>Water from rain or snowmelt flowing over the ground. Damage from it is usually excluded as flood, not covered as backup.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-T">Tolling</dt><dd>Pausing a deadline for a period of time. Michigan’s suit limitation is generally tolled from notice of loss until a formal denial.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-U">Umpire</dt><dd>A neutral person chosen by the two appraisers, or by a court if they can’t agree, who decides items the appraisers can’t settle.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Unfair claims practices</dt><dd>Conduct barred by Michigan’s Uniform Trade Practices Act, such as failing to promptly investigate or pay claims.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-W">Wear and tear</dt><dd>Gradual deterioration from age and use. Policies generally exclude it, and insurers sometimes cite it to deny or reduce roof claims.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-X">Xactimate</dt><dd>Estimating software widely used by insurers, adjusters and contractors that prices repairs line by line using regional price lists.</dd></div></dl><p class="gloss-none" hidden>No terms match that search.</p></article>
